Otitis Externa
Opthalmology & ENT
Tragus/pinna tenderness, Edematous ear canal, Purulent otorrhea

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-029
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, agonizing unilateral ear pain and acute purulent discharge preventing sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed severe, acute unilateral ear pain exacerbated by chewing, alongside rapid onset of purulent otorrhea. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to rule out malignant otitis externa (a life-threatening osteomyelitis in susceptible patients) or acute mastoiditis, and to perform aural toileting to allow topical antibiotics to penetrate the severely swollen canal before routine services opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My ear canal is completely swollen shut, it's oozing yellow pus, and even touching the outside of my ear sends shooting pain into my jaw."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced gradually 48 hours ago as intense pruritus within the left ear canal, progressing rapidly over the last 12 hours to severe, unrelenting pain. Patient reports the pain is agonizing upon chewing or lying on the affected side. Endorses scanty to copious purulent otorrhea and a sensation of profound aural fullness. Notes muffled, conductive hearing loss. Reports recent daily swimming and aggressive use of cotton swabs to dry the ears. Denies systemic fever, post-auricular swelling, or dizziness.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, pacing, unable to sleep or lie down.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; exhausted from pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Distressed, holding hand cupped over the left ear, guarding strictly against any touch.
Physical Exam: Exquisite, severe pain elicited upon light palpation of the tragus and gentle traction of the pinna. Otoscopy reveals a markedly edematous and erythematous external auditory canal, narrowed to 20% of its normal diameter. Thick, yellow purulent debris obscures the deeper canal; the tympanic membrane cannot be visualized due to the swelling and debris. No post-auricular erythema, tenderness, or swelling (negative mastoid signs). Mild, tender preauricular lymphadenopathy is present. Right ear is completely normal.
Environmental Observations: Box of cotton swabs prominently on the bathroom counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Otitis Externa (Swimmer's Ear) - Status: Acute, Severe
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media with Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exquisite pain on pinna traction and tragal pressure (hallmarks of external canal inflammation), whereas purely middle ear infections lack external tenderness.

Mastoiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of post-auricular erythema, absence of a protruding pinna, and lack of systemic toxicity or high fever.

Malignant Otitis Externa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient being young, immunocompetent, and non-diabetic, lacking the deep, boring nocturnal pain and cranial nerve palsies associated with skull base osteomyelitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe, agonizing otalgia and an obstructed, discharging ear canal. Immediate physical assessment was necessary to exclude invasive bone infection (mastoiditis) and to establish the precise anatomical location of the infection. The physical exam confirmed severe acute otitis externa, driven by water exposure and mechanical trauma. Because the canal was severely edematous and filled with debris, delaying care would have allowed the infection to worsen and prolonged the excruciating pain. Immediate aural toileting and the initiation of potent topical antibiotic/corticosteroid drops were required to break the inflammatory cycle and provide safe, localized analgesia overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle dry mopping of the external canal using tissue wicks to remove gross purulent debris. Prescribed Ciprofloxacin 0.3% / Dexamethasone 0.1% ear drops, 5 drops BD for 7 days. Advised oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS and Paracetamol 1g QID for severe analgesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure the topical medication actually reaches the infected tissue, the patient must lie strictly on their unaffected side, instill the drops, and physically pump the tragus (the cartilage flap in front of the ear) 5 times. The immediate sensation of the cold liquid dropping deeper into the canal confirms successful delivery (positive reinforcement), embedding the correct instillation technique essential for curing the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours to ensure the canal swelling is resolving; an ear wick may be required if drops cannot penetrate.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the redness and swelling spread behind the ear, if the ear begins to stick out from the head, if facial weakness develops, or if a high fever over 39C occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a localized skin infection of the ear canal; requires strict adherence to drops.
Clinical Course: The severe pain will begin to drastically improve within 48 hours as the steroid drop shrinks the swelling. Complete resolution takes 7-10 days.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the bone behind your ear becomes red, swollen, and tender, or if your face starts to droop on that side, proceed to the emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Water and cotton buds damaged the protective skin inside your ear canal, allowing a severe bacterial infection to set in. The drops contain an antibiotic to kill the bacteria and a strong steroid to shrink the painful swelling.
Actionable Steps: Throw away all cotton buds immediately. Keep the ear strictly dry when showering by using a cotton ball coated in petroleum jelly. Pump the tragus to push the drops deep into the canal.

SMS INFORMATION

